description:
## Location
- /Volumes/Attic/Desktop/Projects/crates/schc/src/fragment/no_ack.rs lines 102-110

## Problem
When the number of fragments exceeds the FCN range, the sender wraps FCN values:

```rust
let fcn = if is_last {
    fcn_max::<N>() // All-1
} else {
    let max_fcn = fcn_max::<N>() - 1;
    let offset = index as u8;
    if offset > max_fcn {
        // Wrap around if more fragments than FCN range
        max_fcn - (offset % (max_fcn + 1))
    } else {
        max_fcn - offset
    }
};
```

For N=3, FCN range is 0-6 (7 is All-1), so max_fcn=6.
For 10 fragments: FCN sequence would be 6,5,4,3,2,1,0,6,5,4 then All-1.

The receiver has no way to distinguish fragment 0 (FCN=6) from fragment 7 (also FCN=6).

## What Goes Wrong
A receiver implementing RFC 8724 No-Ack mode expects FCN to count down uniquely. If fragments wrap:
1. Fragment 7 (FCN=6) could overwrite fragment 0 in a bitmap-based receiver
2. Fragment ordering becomes ambiguous
3. Reassembly produces corrupt data

RFC 8724 Section 8.4.1 does not define behavior when packet exceeds FCN capacity.

## Suggested Fix
1. Validate at construction that payload fits within FCN range: `fragment_count <= fcn_max::<N>()`
2. Return `Err(FragmentError::BufferTooSmall)` if packet is too large
3. Document the maximum packet size for each N configuration
